.TH auto-upgrade-base 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-upgrade-base - Upgrade to a new release of the base system

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-upgrade-base [release]
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
release     Optional new release, otherwise the user is prompted
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-upgrade-base
command automates upgrades of the base system to a new release.
In contrast, auto-update-system(8) installs updates to the
base system, but does not perform major upgrades.  Some systems
underlying auto-update-system(8),
such as RHEL's "yum update" will upgrade the base to a new
minor version, but not a new major version.
Others, such as FreeBSD's freebsd-update(8),
only install bug fixes and security updates by default, never
upgrading to even a new minor release.

Upgrading a traditional FreeBSD system involves running
"freebsd-update upgrade -r new-release", rebooting, and running
freebsd-update again after the reboot.  In addition, an upgrade
involves switching to new package repositories for add-on
packages, so a "pkg upgrade" is needed to reinstall all
packages to ensure compatibility with the new release.
Different methods may be employed on FreeBSD systems using
pkgbase(7).

The
.B auto-upgrade-base
command handles all of this automatically, ensuring that the
system is properly upgraded and ready for use without any
unnecessary user intervention.

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
auto-upgrade-base
auto-upgrade-base 15.1-RELEASE
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), auto-update-system(8)

